  <span class="os-title-label">Figure </span>
<span class="os-number">2.14</span>



  <span class="os-divider"> </span>
  <span class="os-caption">The symbol for an atom indicates the element via its usual two-letter symbol, the mass number as a left superscript, the atomic number as a left subscript (sometimes omitted), and the charge as a right superscript.</span>
</figcaption></figure></div>
<p id="fs-idm198096624">Information about the naturally occurring isotopes of elements with atomic numbers 1 through 10 is given in <a href="2-3-atomic-structure-and-symbolism#fs-idm87646592" class="autogenerated-content" aria-label="View Table 2.4">Table 2.4</a>. Note that in addition to standard names and symbols, the isotopes of hydrogen are often referred to using common names and accompanying symbols. Hydrogen-2, symbolized <sup>2</sup>H, is also called deut